WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.560
Of all the animals that live on our planet, one extraordinary group dominates.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:20.760
It has produced the largest, the fastest and the most intelligent creatures that have ever

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:21.760
lived.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:33.000
They're known as the vertebrates and they all share one vital feature, a backbone.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:41.640
Now I want to travel back in time to explore their ancient origins and investigate the

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.280
key advances that led to their amazing success.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.160
Things that can also reveal how we came to acquire the characteristic features of our

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.640
own vertebrate bodies.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.480
Jaws that bite, lungs that breathe, ears that can hear.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:06.080
Because the story of the rise of animals is also the story of how you and I came to be

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.600
as we are.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:17.640
I will find evidence in a series of spectacular fossil discoveries around the world and within

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:23.360
living animals.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:31.920
With the latest scientific analysis, we can bring our ancient ancestors back to life and

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:38.560
understand how over 500 million years they developed the bodily features needed to master

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.800
the seas.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.880
Colonize the land and take to the skies.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:02:16.320
This is the story of the rise of animals.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.840
The history of life on earth has been known in outline for many years, but there were

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:28.760
a number of tantalizing gaps in it, particularly in the history of animals with backbones.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.640
When for example did the first signs of a backbone appear?

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:39.920
And is it really true that dinosaurs developed feathers and turned into birds?

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:46.120
Well in recent decades, answers have been found to those extraordinary questions.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:54.840
They're in China and I'm here to look at them.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:02.000
China is the new frontier for fossil discoveries.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.280
Excavations here are unearthing links in the story of the vertebrates that have so far

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:15.320
eluded us.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.960
I have long wanted to see this sensational evidence of myself.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:30.680
I will be traveling to the frozen north of the country and to the capital Beijing.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:42.280
But to search for the first step in our journey, I'm heading south to Yunnan province.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:47.140
This is the site of a thrilling discovery that has given us new evidence for the very

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:54.680
first vertebrates.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:04:01.320
Excavators here are exposing a rich seam of rocks known as the Chongjiang fossil beds.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:07.880
Remarkably they contain the remains of creatures that once swam in the ancient seas 525 million

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.240
years ago.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:17.240
Paleontologist Ho Xiangguang was the first to discover the unique features of these beds,

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:29.080
an astonishing perfection of preservation.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:35.280
To find complete bodies like this is extremely rare.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:41.200
When an animal dies in the sea, normally bacteria destroy the soft parts very quickly,

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:47.120
so all that we can find afterwards are the hard parts, bone or shell.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.640
Why that didn't happen here in this particular part of this particular sea is something of

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:52.640
a mystery.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.360
It may be something to do with the lack of oxygen, but whatever it was, it has given

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.840
us a privileged view into one of the most exciting chapters in the whole history of

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:06.640
life.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:14.080
The beds have so far yielded over 200 separate species.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.240
This was a time period known as the Cambrian.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:25.080
The land was still bare and lifeless, but underwater it was exploding into a multitude

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:34.420
of forms.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:39.280
The major animal groups we know today were appearing on the planet for the very first

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.600
time.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.980
They built their bodies entirely of soft tissue.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:57.360
Some protected and supported it with a hard outer casing, but none had anything that resembled

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.560
a backbone.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:07.840
These were the invertebrates.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:13.160
Then Professor Ho and his team found one intriguing exception.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:23.160
It's a fossil called Millo cunmingia, but to examine it in detail, you've got to look

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.640
at it under the microscope.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:37.280
Its features reveal evidence of a new type of support not outside the body, but inside.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:47.720
This is one of about 30 specimens that have already been found of this tiny little creature.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:56.160
Under the microscope, it contains an extraordinary amount of detail.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:02.160
Those marks are marks that have been made by the excavator's needle.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.160
This is the animal itself.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:11.440
This is its head, the top of its back, and nearly every one of them have these two little

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.000
black spots at the front, eye spots.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:23.080
Looking farther down the animal, there are just some striations here, little bars, which

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:28.480
are thought to have been the gill bars, the little constructions that carry blood vessels

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.880
which enabled the animal to extract oxygen from the waters it flowed over and breathe.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:44.240
And behind them, farther down the animal, there are these bars, bands of muscle, and

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:51.320
they were probably attached to a grisly rod somewhere in the middle there.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:59.880
This is called a notochord, which was the forerunner of the backbone.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:07.880
Millo con mingia is the earliest creature we know of that we can truly call a vertebrate.

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:15.700
And it seems clear that it used its strong inner rod to move in an entirely new way.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:20.360
As the muscles contract, they bend the rod from side to side.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:34.960
This movement pushes against the water and creates forward thrust.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:38.560
Here was a revolutionary new way to get around.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:44.480
It allowed Millo con mingia to roam far and wide and escape the dangerous invertebrate

00:08:44.480 --> 00:09:00.440
predators that were prowling the seas.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:06.200
The vertebrates would diversify over millions of years to create the spectacular variety

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:19.080
of backbone creatures we see today in every environment on the planet.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:23.880
Fish dominate the seas, lakes, and rivers.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.320
The amphibians live in both water and land.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:33.000
The reptiles can survive in the driest places on Earth.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.760
The birds rule the skies.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:45.240
And the mammals have insulated their bodies to adapt to every climate.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:53.480
We humans have used our greater intelligence to overrun the planet.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:59.120
This astonishing journey was built on a series of key evolutionist steps that helped our

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:07.760
ancient ancestors to exploit their environments and overcome huge challenges.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:17.520
The first of these advances was the development of that inner support, the notochord.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:26.900
Back in Europe, you can find a creature that represents the next critical step in our story.

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:32.480
It lives unobtrusively and often ignored in British rivers.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.720
And it sheds light on the challenges those first vertebrates faced.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:46.940
Ah, there it is.

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:50.320
This is a lamprey.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:57.040
You might think at first sight that it was a kind of fish, but it's not.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:02.280
It's something much, much more primitive.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:05.560
It has no fins.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:13.040
And even its tail is nothing more than a flattened blade.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:20.720
But what is most remarkable about it is that it doesn't really have a true mouth.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:27.000
Its mouth is just a simple hole with little bristles about it.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:34.640
And it feeds by sucking in water through that mouth and then filtering out little particles

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:38.320
of food.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:44.680
So this little animal takes us right back to the time when the first animals with backbones

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:46.920
appeared on Earth.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:57.440
It's a true living fossil.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:02.360
The first vertebrates seemed to have had the same kind of mouth, and they were almost certainly

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:07.840
limited to the same kind of simple food.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:13.680
Over time, other forms evolved with different shapes and sizes, many of them rather larger

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:15.640
than Millicommingae.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:20.960
But all of them had that very simple mouth, an opening at the front of the body as the

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:23.040
lamprey has today.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:27.280
If the early vertebrates were going to really take advantage of the variety of food that

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:32.760
was available in those early seas, they were going to have to develop a much more complex

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:39.640
and powerful form of eating machinery.

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:44.400
Scientists on the East Coast of the United States are seeing evidence of this evolutionary

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:52.240
advance not in fossils, but in living creatures.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:13:04.120
In New England.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:08.920
Marine biologists at the University of New England are studying a group of fish with

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:12.520
a very ancient ancestry.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:17.360
They build their skeletons with the same strong material that formed the grisly rod of the

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:21.280
first vertebrates, cartilage.

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:27.400
Where the sharks, skates and rays.

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:33.880
This group appeared among the vertebrates over 420 million years ago.

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:39.280
And that means we can use them to examine the development before that split of a remarkable

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:43.600
piece of engineering that changed the course of evolutionary history.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:53.200
The jaw.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:57.800
If you look back on the evolutionary tree, you'd find that a jaw is a really important

00:13:57.800 --> 00:13:58.960
feature to have.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:05.240
And it's one of the features that have made skates and sharks apex predators in the environments

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:08.880
in which they live.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:15.480
A jaw hinged to the skull brought the new ability to grab food, then rip or grind it

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:19.080
into digestible pieces.

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:24.360
But where did this amazing piece of equipment come from?

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:31.720
Scientists have found an answer by studying the way living vertebrates develop as embryos.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:38.160
Skates lay their fertilized eggs on the seabed inside leathery cases called mermaid's purses.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:44.160
Scientists can open these up and observe them as they develop, fed by a generous supply

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:49.280
of egg yolk.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:54.960
This skate embryo has a simple structure shared by all embryonic vertebrates that served as

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.760
the basis of the first jaw.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:02.840
What we see are these folds.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:09.640
And what's really interesting about this is that this skate is in about four months of its development.

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:15.040
And if we take a close look at another vertebrate, we can see it looks very similar.

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:19.240
Here we have the head, and it's going to follow down to the body.

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:21.640
You also see the folds.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:27.880
Now, this is actually a human being.

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:32.600
It's thought that the embryos of the earliest vertebrates looked much like this, and that

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:38.400
each fold developed into a gill.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:44.520
In a skate embryo, the folds furthest from the head keep their original purpose and form

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:47.800
the rigid arches of its gills.

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:55.200
But the nearest fold has been adapted to form an upper and lower jaw.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:00.360
In a human embryo, the lower folds develop into structures that include the larynx and

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:04.600
the throat.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:14.400
But the top fold once again constructs the jaw.

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:19.680
The development of the jaw improved the ability to collect food.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:24.960
And those that lacked it, with a few exceptions like the lamprey, died out.

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:30.400
But in order to collect food, you have to find it, and that led to an improvement in

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:35.080
swimming.

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:41.520
In the Chinese capital, Beijing, I've been given special access to a newly identified

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:44.800
missing link.

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:50.640
This tiny fossil holds clues that are just fragments and hard to spot, but it's the

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:59.440
earliest example yet found of a creature with two pairs of fins.

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:05.800
It's called Paraunana lepis, and it's about 410 million years old.

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:10.920
Its front part, like many other fish at the time, was covered by armor plating to protect

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:20.600
it from predators.

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:26.240
Underneath, you can see that it has a mouth.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:32.360
And although the lower jaw is missing, you can tell from marks on the upper jaw that

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:34.240
it was once there.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:41.400
But what is most important about this is its fins.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:45.700
Just their stumps are visible.

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:52.880
It had two fins at the front, petrel fins.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:58.760
They were shaped rather like the wings of an aeroplane, and they had the same effect,

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:03.100
creating upwards lift through the water.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:06.080
Front fins have been found on older fish.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:12.440
But what is interesting is this is the earliest example which has another pair of fins at

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:18.720
the back, the pelvic fins.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:24.240
This second, smaller pair brought much more stability, helping the fish to hold its course

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:29.960
through the water.

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:37.080
This system was hugely successful, and it made the sharks the skillful swimmers that

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.980
they are today.

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:51.040
So now the vertebrates had jaws and four fins.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:57.280
To see evidence of the next crucial development, I'm heading out onto Lake Fuxian in southern

00:18:57.280 --> 00:19:02.560
China.

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:08.160
These waters are home to living descendants of a group that developed a new kind of inner

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:18.920
support, a support that would have huge significance for later life.

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:23.480
Here they get a lot of fish like this.

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:24.960
It's a carp.

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:29.600
And it's very different from the sharks we've been looking at, because instead of having

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:35.120
cartilage skeletons, carp and others like it have skeletons that are strengthened with

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:36.120
calcium phosphate.

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:43.440
They are bony, and most fish today have buried skeletons.

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:51.320
Bone contains the main material found in cartilage, a long stringy protein called collagen.

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:56.200
Hard crystals of calcium phosphate add strength.

00:19:56.200 --> 00:20:08.840
But the collagen can still flex slightly and prevent the bone snapping under pressure.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:15.120
These bony fish could subject their skeletons to the far greater forces that come from increases

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:20.840
in speed and agility.

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:34.160
They added mobile fan-shaped fins and assumed a multitude of different forms.

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:40.600
From their simple origins over 500 million years ago, the sharks and bony fish diversified

00:20:40.600 --> 00:20:49.000
to dominate every underwater environment on Earth.

00:20:49.000 --> 00:21:02.760
There are over 35,000 species alive today.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:08.720
The strong inner bony support to the body had evolved in water, but it would prove most

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:22.760
spectacularly successful in a completely new environment.

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:29.520
For most of the Earth's history until now, the land had been empty and barren.

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:37.040
But around 450 million years ago, first plants, then worms, and then the ancestors of insects

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:40.160
began to colonize it.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:45.040
Here were rich pickings for any vertebrate that could reach them.

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:52.280
The stage was set for one of the most astonishing leaps in evolutionary history.

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:58.600
The vertebrates move onto land.

00:21:58.600 --> 00:22:06.480
But to achieve this remarkable feat, they would need to make a major modification.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:12.480
To move around on land without the support of water, these fish needed a way to lift

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:15.760
their bodies up from the surface of the ground.

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:20.560
They needed limbs.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:28.280
Scientists have recently found the earliest evidence for this key moment in Eastern Europe.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:37.720
Zahelmia, Poland.

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:45.880
Once a quarry for building stone, today this is a hugely significant fossil site.

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:51.480
But paleontologist Per Aalberg and his team aren't looking for bodies.

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:58.880
They're looking for the marks the bodies left behind.

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:08.720
393 million years ago, this was the soft, muddy floor of a tropical lagoon.

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:13.520
You can still see mud cracks here from an episode when the lagoon dried out and the

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:16.960
mud all flaked up.

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:21.560
For millions of years, the mud solidified into layers of rock, which were then tilted

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:26.920
by movements in the earth's crust.

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:32.600
By carefully exposing each layer, Per and his team have been able to uncover a series

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:36.280
of intriguing tracks.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:38.120
There are three big dimples in the rock.

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:45.080
There's one here, one here, and one down by my feet.

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:51.160
These are not erosional hollows.

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:53.040
It's not like rock has been scooped away.

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:56.340
Something's been pressed into the surface of the mud while it was still soft.

00:23:56.340 --> 00:24:00.420
You can see that from the internal texture here, but also from the fact that you've

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:06.520
got a slightly raised rim around the edge where the mud has been displaced.

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:12.640
So a large, heavy animal, presumably a vertebrate of some sort, pushed an appendage into the

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:18.200
mud here once, twice, three times in succession.

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:28.400
The marks suggest a creature floating and pushing itself around in the shallows.

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:33.800
But Per and his team have found a more detailed set of prints that show an animal doing something

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:38.320
even more radical.

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:42.180
This is one of the most important specimens from the entire site, and the reason for that

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.440
is the pattern that these prints make.

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:48.400
You can see easily, I think, that they make pairs, one in front of another, in this kind

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:50.400
of diagonal arrangement.

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:56.440
In order to be able to produce this, you need to have limbs that stick out to the side and

00:24:56.440 --> 00:25:00.080
which can be swung forwards and backwards rather freely while you're flexing your body

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:01.080
from side to side.

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:03.440
Then you can generate this kind of pattern.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:10.160
A fish crawling trace would not look like this.

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:15.080
Another extraordinary slab has even preserved the imprint of what Per believes is a fully

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:21.480
formed foot, complete with toes.

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:28.120
So how did the vertebrates make this astonishing transition from fish swimming to animals with

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:33.360
four legs walking on land?

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:38.200
In search of clues, I'm heading to London and the Natural History Museum, home to the

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:46.000
largest collection of plant and animal specimens in the world.

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:51.160
I'm here to see the remains of an ancient creature once hailed as a missing link that

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:57.280
would answer such questions.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:00.720
This is a type of bony fish called a coelacanth.

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:08.720
Its fossilized skeletons have been found in rocks even older than those in Poland.

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:14.480
Its fins have an intriguing feature not seen in other kinds of fish.

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:20.480
Their base is a rounded fleshy stump that looks tantalizingly like the beginnings of

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:23.320
a leg.

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:30.640
So scientists thought that this might well be the ancestor of all land-living vertebrates.

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:32.880
And then a sensation.

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:39.040
A living coelacanth was hauled up from the depths of the Indian Ocean and the museum

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:42.960
has acquired several of them.

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:48.200
Here is the body of a baby coelacanth.

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:53.680
The coelacanth female retains the egg in her body until it's fully developed.

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:56.960
There's its yolk sac and here's its fin.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:27:01.800
And you can see this fleshy base to it here and then its fin rays.

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:08.720
The question is, was that strong enough to enable a fish like this to haul itself out

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:16.720
of the water and up onto land?

00:27:16.720 --> 00:27:23.560
Now living coelacanths have been filmed in the depths of the sea.

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:31.000
Its fleshy muscular fins do certainly help it to maneuver its five foot long body.

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:38.000
There is even the hint of a walking pattern.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:48.260
But detailed analysis has revealed that their fins are still a long way from being legs.

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:54.220
The ancient coelacanth marked a crucial early stage in that transition, but some characteristics

00:27:54.220 --> 00:28:02.840
rule it out as a direct ancestor of the land vertebrates.

00:28:02.840 --> 00:28:10.440
All land-living backbone animals have limbs which have a basic similar bone structure.

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:18.840
There is one bone at the top, then there are two bones and a group of bones followed with

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:21.600
digits.

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:25.680
And the coelacanths didn't have that structure.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:34.600
And then recently another fossil discovery was made.

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:42.440
Ellesmere Island lies in the icy waters between Northern Canada and Greenland.

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:50.440
A team of American paleontologists who shot this footage believed that the rocks here

00:28:50.440 --> 00:28:57.800
were deposited in the right sort of environment for the vertebrates moved to land.

00:28:57.800 --> 00:29:03.680
We learned about a sequence of rocks that formed in ancient stream systems and our hypothesis

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:17.460
was that it was in those sorts of environments where limbs were being favoured over fins.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:24.640
The arrival of plants on land had stimulated a surge in life in and around freshwater swamps

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:32.060
and this created new opportunities for the fish that lived here.

00:29:32.060 --> 00:29:39.160
One of the niches that was being developed at that time was for shallow water predators.

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:45.760
Which fish could find other fish that were living in the shallows, the swamps, the productive

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:52.000
ecosystems that were just starting to appear on earth at that time.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:57.220
Ted Dachla and his colleagues believed that limb-like fins could have helped a fish to

00:29:57.220 --> 00:30:03.040
hunt in this kind of environment.

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:13.760
And then on the slopes of a barren valley they made a thrilling discovery.

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:17.680
This was the fossil that got us really excited.

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:21.360
We couldn't have dreamed actually that we would find something as well preserved as

00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:22.360
this one.

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:26.700
It's about the front two thirds or half of the body.

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:33.080
As you can see a very complete skull and a large piece of the body including parts of

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:35.960
the fin.

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:43.080
The team found features that matched the profile of a shallow water predator.

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:53.880
Eyes placed on the top of a flattened head and ranks of sharp teeth.

00:30:53.880 --> 00:31:01.900
They gave it a local Inuit name Tik Tarnik.

00:31:01.900 --> 00:31:20.200
We can now work out from its bones how Tik Tarnik moved around in those swamps and shallows.

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:29.560
In deep water it must have swum like any other fish.

00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:34.740
But further examination of its bones showed that it could also move its body in a far

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:39.040
more radical way.

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:44.840
One of the really amazing aspects of Tik Tarnik that we've noticed is this evolution of the

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:46.060
neck.

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:50.760
There was not a rigid connection between the skull and the rest of the body.

00:31:50.760 --> 00:31:56.840
Tik Tarnik is the first vertebrate we see that has freed up the neck and when you think

00:31:56.840 --> 00:32:02.840
about it all limbed animals including ourselves would not be able to move our head independently

00:32:02.840 --> 00:32:08.360
of our shoulders if it were not for these innovations that were occurring in a form

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:10.840
like Tik Tarnik.

00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:16.920
The flexible neck allowed Tik Tarnik to point its jaws at its prey when space was too cramped

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:20.440
to maneuver its whole body.

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:30.240
But it was the fins that provided the team with the most exciting evidence.

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:36.780
Behind the spiny rays there were lobe-like stumps like those of the coelacanth.

00:32:36.780 --> 00:32:42.520
But Tik Tarnik's bones revealed a pattern that was much closer to the basic structure

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:45.720
of limbs.

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:52.000
We learned a lot about the fin of Tik Tarnik from this specimen.

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:57.440
Now this is a cast of all the different bones that we found in association including the

00:32:57.440 --> 00:33:00.840
shoulder girdle here.

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:04.360
But that is the complete fin skeleton from the front fin.

00:33:04.360 --> 00:33:12.480
So I'm a lobe fin fish, here is my front fin, call it a limb now, but here is Tik Tarnik's

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:13.480
front fin.

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:19.840
We've got a shoulder joint and it's very important that there's a shoulder joint which is oriented

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:23.760
a little bit laterally, a little bit down in Tik Tarnik.

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:28.620
Very different from an animal that's just swimming with its fin and paddling along.

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:31.840
This fin seemed to be oriented beneath the body.

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:33.120
So this is the humerus.

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:38.280
We all have a humerus, that's the first bone in the front appendage.

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.360
We have a ulna and a radius.

00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:45.200
So you and I, all limbed animals, have an ulna and radius.

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:51.200
We have some wrist bones and we actually then have something which like a wrist could bend

00:33:51.200 --> 00:33:59.600
together and allow this fin to sit down and to contact the surface with the surface area.

00:33:59.600 --> 00:34:05.040
And so when we see all of these features, we see a structure which is very much like

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:06.240
our limbs.

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:13.560
So here is a fish using its fin in a very limb-like way.

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:20.560
Tik Tarnik's heavy duty fin still helped it to swim.

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:30.120
But if it hit the shallows, the bones and joints would help to push itself up and punt

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:31.360
around.

00:34:31.360 --> 00:34:42.000
But this new limb didn't just help mobility in the water.

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:50.920
It became the driving force behind one of the most spectacular events in evolutionary

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:51.920
history.

00:34:51.920 --> 00:35:18.160
The first vertebrate animals on land.

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:31.760
Over time, creatures evolved that spent most of their time out of water.

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:35.200
They formed a new group we call amphibians.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:39.480
And to survive on land, they had to solve a new challenge.

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:45.000
They had to be able to extract oxygen not from water like their fish ancestors, but

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:48.240
from the air.

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:55.480
Fish use gills to absorb oxygen into the body.

00:35:55.480 --> 00:36:04.160
In air, gills quickly dry out and stop working.

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:09.320
China is the home of a rare and fascinating creature that can show us how the ancient

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:14.000
amphibians overcame this problem.

00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:26.080
Today, the biggest amphibian alive is this creature, the Chinese giant salamander.

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:34.760
It breathes partly through its skin, which has these long flaps on it, and that absorbs

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:38.880
oxygen from the water.

00:36:38.880 --> 00:36:45.640
But it also breathes air.

00:36:45.640 --> 00:36:58.760
It's going to come up, and as it does, it snatches a gulp of air, blows a few bubbles,

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:04.000
and sinks down again.

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:09.480
Its jaw acts as a pump, forcing air down into the body.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:18.120
Here, oxygen is absorbed into the bloodstream from two inflatable sacks with permeable walls,

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:21.480
lungs.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:27.480
Because they're enclosed inside the body, they don't dry out.

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:33.520
The lungs it uses are just simple pouches coming from the back of the throat.

00:37:33.520 --> 00:37:40.800
Nonetheless, they were the first kind of lungs that animals had, the forerunners of the air-breathing

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:58.960
organs that all of us land-living vertebrates now have.

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:05.240
In their origins around 365 million years ago, the amphibians took on many different

00:38:05.240 --> 00:38:09.040
forms.

00:38:09.040 --> 00:38:21.840
Over 7,000 species now live in a variety of habitats, on land and in water.

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:35.960
They include salamanders, frogs, and newts.

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:38.920
But two things tied the amphibians to water.

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:44.240
First, their skins are moist, and if they dry out, they die.

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:51.360
And secondly, their eggs, like this frog's form, are covered in nothing more than jelly,

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:56.440
and they have to be laid in water, or at the very least, in moist conditions.

00:38:56.440 --> 00:39:02.000
And until the vertebrates could solve those two problems, they would not be able to colonise

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:11.520
the dry parts of the land.

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:19.520
Then a group of pioneers appeared, with an amazing new feature to their bodies.

00:39:19.520 --> 00:39:24.000
You can find the evidence for this next step by looking at animals that can survive far

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:31.880
from water today.

00:39:31.880 --> 00:39:34.520
This little creature is a lizard.

00:39:34.520 --> 00:39:38.000
They call it in these parts a tree dragon.

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:42.440
And its body is very much the same shape as an amphibian.

00:39:42.440 --> 00:39:47.120
Long body with the backbone and two pairs of limbs.

00:39:47.120 --> 00:39:51.440
But there's one crucial difference between an animal like this, an amphibian.

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:53.480
Its skin is not moist.

00:39:53.480 --> 00:39:56.840
It's dry.

00:39:56.840 --> 00:40:04.120
We can see what has changed by putting the two types of skin under the microscope.

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:11.560
The skin of an amphibian is smooth with living cells visible on the surface.

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:17.480
A lizard skin is much rougher because it contains large amounts of keratin, a protein

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:24.600
similar to that from which our own fingernails are formed.

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:33.740
Keratin-filled cells dry out and layer up to form scales.

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:40.560
This creates a barrier, sealing water inside the body.

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:46.600
We humans have inherited this keratin barrier in our skin, allowing us to maintain up to

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:54.480
70% of our bodies as water.

00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:57.680
Animals with this body plan became a huge success.

00:40:57.680 --> 00:41:02.200
They evolved into a great number of species, big and small.

00:41:02.200 --> 00:41:07.240
We call them reptiles.

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:21.120
But the reptiles still had to overcome a second challenge, how to lay their eggs out of water.

00:41:21.120 --> 00:41:27.160
I have come to Lufeng in southern China to see evidence gathered by local scientists

00:41:27.160 --> 00:41:35.920
of the ingenious solution.

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:43.680
These eggs were laid by a reptile, and as you might imagine, a pretty big one at that.

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:49.120
The first reptilian eggs almost certainly had a leathery covering, rather like those

00:41:49.120 --> 00:41:51.520
a turtle lays today.

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:54.020
But these eggs are different.

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:58.200
They have a hard covering, a shell.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:02.840
And you can see where the weight of the sand that eventually covered them and fossilized

00:42:02.840 --> 00:42:10.600
them bore down upon them, they crushed that shell, but the pieces are still in place.

00:42:10.600 --> 00:42:15.520
From examining modern reptile eggs, we know that this shell must have been made of hard

00:42:15.520 --> 00:42:21.320
calcium carbonate, and it must have supported an inner fibrous membrane.

00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:25.760
Together, they made the egg watertight.

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:31.340
And that meant that the animals that laid them no longer had to go back to the water

00:42:31.340 --> 00:42:34.640
to lay their eggs, as all amphibians had to do.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:41.600
Instead, they could go to the driest part of the land and breed and nest and lay their

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:42.600
eggs.

00:42:42.600 --> 00:42:50.160
So all the dry land was open to them.

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:55.160
The amphibians had spearheaded the move to land.

00:42:55.160 --> 00:43:02.160
Now their descendants, the reptiles, were able to establish themselves in its driest

00:43:02.160 --> 00:43:08.200
parts.

00:43:08.200 --> 00:43:19.400
Over 9,500 species now inhabit our planet.

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:24.200
But the limbs that helped the vertebrates emerge from the water began to present problems

00:43:24.200 --> 00:43:28.280
when it came to walking efficiently on dry land.

00:43:28.280 --> 00:43:33.360
Because they projected sideways, it took a lot of effort to hold their bodies off the

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:36.880
ground.

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:46.360
Then around 230 million years ago, one set of reptiles developed an amazing solution.

00:43:46.360 --> 00:43:52.560
These eggs were laid by an animal belonging to the most successful of all reptile groups,

00:43:52.560 --> 00:44:19.520
a group that dominated the world for 100 million years, the dinosaurs.

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:25.540
More than 150 different species of dinosaur have been found in the rocks of China alone,

00:44:25.540 --> 00:44:36.080
and over a thousand worldwide.

00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:42.280
And they too depended on a crucial advance, a radical modification of the bone that connects

00:44:42.280 --> 00:44:51.040
the leg to the body, the hip.

00:44:51.040 --> 00:44:56.760
This is Lufungosaurus, a plant eater.

00:44:56.760 --> 00:45:02.160
The early reptiles had legs which sprayed out from either side of the body and left

00:45:02.160 --> 00:45:04.560
the body very close to the ground.

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:09.600
But a change in the shape of the hips of the dinosaurs enabled them to bring their hind

00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:16.000
legs underneath the body and so lift it up and give them greater freedom of movement.

00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:21.400
And some of them, including Lufungosaurus, were able to support the entire weight of

00:45:21.400 --> 00:45:28.400
the body on the hind legs.

00:45:28.400 --> 00:45:34.640
This new hip, along with sturdier leg joints, allowed the dinosaurs to take longer strides

00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:43.160
and support heavier bodies.

00:45:43.160 --> 00:45:48.920
They became the largest animals that have ever lived on land.

00:45:48.920 --> 00:45:55.440
But this new way of walking was also the first step on the road to an even more radical evolutionary

00:45:55.440 --> 00:45:58.760
advance.

00:45:58.760 --> 00:46:05.880
It was from this group of two-legged dinosaurs that there came a truly astonishing development

00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:10.680
that we are only just beginning to understand and that was to lift the vertebrates to a

00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:15.360
completely new level.

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:21.160
The backbone animals had colonized the seas and invaded the land.

00:46:21.160 --> 00:46:28.240
But there was one final habitat to explore, the skies.

00:46:28.240 --> 00:46:33.360
The extraordinary Chinese fossil bed is providing the missing evidence for one of the great

00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:37.680
mysteries in evolutionary science.

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:47.680
The intriguing link between dinosaurs and birds.

00:46:47.680 --> 00:46:54.960
I'm heading for Liaoning province to fulfill a long-held dream and see the sight of these

00:46:54.960 --> 00:47:00.680
discoveries for myself.

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:05.280
These rocks are about 125 million years old.

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:11.120
At that time, this part of China was tropical and the land was covered with a lot of freshwater

00:47:11.120 --> 00:47:19.040
lakes and in those lakes was washed sediment which formed these bands here.

00:47:19.040 --> 00:47:26.200
And every now and again the sediment changes color and that is ash that was spewed out

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:28.400
from a nearby volcano.

00:47:28.400 --> 00:47:34.840
So that about that level there, there were a lot of skeletons awaiting to be discovered.

00:47:34.840 --> 00:47:42.280
And when they were discovered, they revealed some sensational facts about dinosaurs.

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:47.200
The most sensational for a very long time.

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:50.960
This fossil was one of the most remarkable to emerge.

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:55.120
A two-legged dinosaur about the size of a cat.

00:47:55.120 --> 00:48:00.240
It's been named Sinusoropteryx.

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:07.160
Its discovery revealed an intriguing feature never seen before on a dinosaur.

00:48:07.160 --> 00:48:15.560
Up its tail and down its back, a covering of what looks like fur.

00:48:15.560 --> 00:48:21.200
Fresh finds have revealed that a wide range of two-legged dinosaurs had skin covered by

00:48:21.200 --> 00:48:26.120
very similar hair-like filaments.

00:48:26.120 --> 00:48:30.640
But what were they for?

00:48:30.640 --> 00:48:39.760
In Beijing, there are the crucial specimens that answered those questions.

00:48:39.760 --> 00:48:48.280
This is one of the world's leading institutions in the study of dinosaur evolution.

00:48:48.280 --> 00:48:59.240
Professor Xuxing and his colleagues have been analyzing another larger specimen of Sinusoropteryx.

00:48:59.240 --> 00:49:05.040
It too retains traces, just fragments of the mysterious filaments.

00:49:05.040 --> 00:49:15.760
If you look at near the tail, the dark things there near the tail, they are single filaments,

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:19.440
just like our hair, very, very simple.

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:22.960
Xuxing has been puzzling over their function.

00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:27.080
Together, these filaments create a covering like fur.

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:33.120
So the most likely answer is that they serve to keep these dinosaurs warm.

00:49:33.120 --> 00:49:41.240
But detailed examination has suggested an additional and very different function.

00:49:41.240 --> 00:49:49.000
Experts at the institute have taken minute samples and examined them under powerful magnification.

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:53.440
They contain intriguing structures.

00:49:53.440 --> 00:49:59.200
Some are lozenge-shaped, some spherical.

00:49:59.200 --> 00:50:07.720
Investigators identified them as melanosomes, microscopic capsules that contain pigment.

00:50:07.720 --> 00:50:13.880
They would have given the filaments on Sinusoropteryx's tail colour.

00:50:13.880 --> 00:50:22.400
Based on our analysis, you see stripes, one like a white brown, white brown.

00:50:22.400 --> 00:50:23.400
Really?

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:24.400
Yes, definitely.

00:50:24.400 --> 00:50:25.400
It's a beautiful pattern.

00:50:25.400 --> 00:50:30.800
Of course, you can see, oh, that's maybe for display or communication.

00:50:30.800 --> 00:50:33.520
Do we know how it held its tail?

00:50:33.520 --> 00:50:35.600
Tails definitely can move in different directions.

00:50:35.600 --> 00:50:39.400
In most cases, I guess, it's up or horizontal.

00:50:39.400 --> 00:50:46.160
So it's like a wingtail limo, waving its tail round as a display.

00:50:46.160 --> 00:50:51.360
Dinosaurs may have used these coloured furry bands to signal to other members of the species

00:50:51.360 --> 00:50:54.560
or to act as camouflage.

00:50:54.560 --> 00:51:02.960
But then came a discovery that suggested another far more significant function.

00:51:02.960 --> 00:51:08.240
I have been granted privileged access to the underground vaults of the Beijing Museum of

00:51:08.240 --> 00:51:13.160
Natural History to look at one of the most important creatures yet to be found in the

00:51:13.160 --> 00:51:26.680
fossil beds of Liaoning.

00:51:26.680 --> 00:51:53.200
This is Ankyornis, a creature that's clearly a dinosaur.

00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:59.080
It's got powerful legs here, ending with toes with sharp claws on them.

00:51:59.080 --> 00:52:04.440
And its head, which has been detached, lies here, upside down.

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:11.200
But you can see the jaw, which has teeth in them.

00:52:11.200 --> 00:52:17.240
But what is spectacular about this particular specimen is the perfection of the preservation

00:52:17.240 --> 00:52:19.820
of these structures.

00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:26.580
They show that the simple filaments have developed into something far more complex.

00:52:26.580 --> 00:52:31.720
The central stalk has tiny strands branching out on either side.

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:36.960
The filaments have become feathers.

00:52:36.960 --> 00:52:45.960
Analysis of them has shown that the crest here on the head was a rufous red colour,

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:54.040
and the body feathers were striped black and white.

00:52:54.040 --> 00:53:00.840
Their feathers all down the legs.

00:53:00.840 --> 00:53:08.640
And looking at the density of them on the forearms here, it does look very like a wing.

00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:14.120
So the question is, could this animal fly?

00:53:14.120 --> 00:53:25.800
Could this be the moment when a dinosaur became a bird?

00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:30.240
A clue to the answer could come from the environment in which it lived.

00:53:30.240 --> 00:53:38.640
At this time, this area of northern China was covered in lush forests.

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:42.760
Animals that could climb trees would be able to collect food that was not available on

00:53:42.760 --> 00:53:45.520
the ground.

00:53:45.520 --> 00:53:51.120
They could also find safety from ground-living predators.

00:53:51.120 --> 00:53:56.640
Shu Xing and his colleagues see evidence that Ancheonis adapted to a tree-living way of

00:53:56.640 --> 00:54:03.560
life by putting its feathers to a new use.

00:54:03.560 --> 00:54:07.720
Ancheonis has some features suggesting tree-living lifestyle.

00:54:07.720 --> 00:54:14.720
For example, you look at Ancheonis' toe, they have very curved claws.

00:54:14.720 --> 00:54:18.360
And also they have big feathers attached to their feet.

00:54:18.360 --> 00:54:23.880
If Ancheonis is a tree-living animal, then you have a good reason to believe that flight

00:54:23.880 --> 00:54:31.960
starts from tree down, which means the bird's ancestor can take advantage of gravity and

00:54:31.960 --> 00:54:37.480
then start their journey to the sky.

00:54:37.480 --> 00:54:58.680
Because Ancheonis lived high up, it could use its feathers to glide.

00:54:58.680 --> 00:55:04.480
It must have needed all the feathers growing along its front limbs, hind limbs and tail

00:55:04.480 --> 00:55:21.120
to create a large enough surface to catch the air and slow its descent.

00:55:21.120 --> 00:55:27.520
It wasn't capable of flapping flight, but at 160 million years old, it's now the earliest

00:55:27.520 --> 00:55:38.840
creature we know to have used feathers to fly.

00:55:38.840 --> 00:55:45.720
The gliding dinosaurs would eventually give rise to a whole new group of vertebrates,

00:55:45.720 --> 00:55:52.240
the birds.

00:55:52.240 --> 00:55:59.320
Over 9,000 species crowd our skies today.

00:55:59.320 --> 00:56:05.680
An astonishing evolutionary journey had enabled the vertebrates to dominate every corner of

00:56:05.680 --> 00:56:10.840
the planet.

00:56:10.840 --> 00:56:18.040
It was a journey that began in the Cambrian seas over 500 million years ago and that led

00:56:18.040 --> 00:56:26.160
to the development of a set of body parts that we ourselves would ultimately inherit.

00:56:26.160 --> 00:56:31.200
Jaws and a bony skeleton from the early fish.

00:56:31.200 --> 00:56:39.520
Limbs and lungs from the amphibians.

00:56:39.520 --> 00:56:45.080
Water-tight skin from the reptiles.

00:56:45.080 --> 00:56:50.400
By the time the birds appeared on the planet, the early pioneers of another major vertebrate

00:56:50.400 --> 00:56:55.440
group had also evolved.

00:56:55.440 --> 00:57:02.040
At first they were tiny, but they were destined eventually to dominate the earth.

00:57:02.040 --> 00:57:03.040
They were the mammals.

00:57:03.040 --> 00:57:11.480
Most, I dare say, were a little better than the snack food for the dinosaurs, but all

00:57:11.480 --> 00:57:19.400
that was about to change.

00:57:19.400 --> 00:57:28.760
A devastating meteor strike that many believe triggered a mass extinction.

00:57:28.760 --> 00:57:38.080
We don't know exactly what happened, but certainly 65 million years ago all the dinosaurs disappeared.

00:57:38.080 --> 00:57:42.080
But some of the birds and mammals survived.

00:57:42.080 --> 00:57:53.280
And with the bigger dinosaurs gone, the world was up for grabs.

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:59.520
Next time, I'll be investigating the extraordinary rise of the mammals.

00:57:59.520 --> 00:58:05.120
To discover how they developed a remarkable set of new bodily features to become the most

00:58:05.120 --> 00:58:10.560
complex and successful vertebrates yet.

00:58:10.560 --> 00:58:11.840
Powerful senses.

00:58:11.840 --> 00:58:16.320
A radical new way of producing their young.

00:58:16.320 --> 00:58:20.080
And monstrous bodies.

00:58:20.080 --> 00:58:26.560
We will also see how we humans finally arrived on the tree of life, with hugely advanced

00:58:26.560 --> 00:58:40.080
brains that would allow us to out-compete all other species on the planet.

00:58:40.080 --> 00:59:04.000
We are here at vertebrate.
